The prevalence of gardening-introduced non-native plants is strongly and systematically habitat-dependent, forming a clear gradient across the ten most widespread habitat types of Barcelona province. Occupancy prevalence (proportion of sampled plots containing at least one gardening-introduced non-native species) is highest in anthropogenic and moisture-rich habitats -- Wetlands (50% of plots, 95% Wilson CI 35-65%), Urban (48%, 36-60%), Coastal (45%, 28-62%), Riparian (36%, 25-49%) -- intermediate in Croplands (21%, 14-30%), and very low in Meadows (8%), Coniferous forests (6.5%), Broad-leaved forests (4.4%), Scrublands (1.9%) and Rock outcrops (0%). The habitat effect is highly significant overall (chi-square=139.4, df=9, p=1.3e-25, Cramer's V=0.47; logistic McFadden R2=0.24) and persists after controlling for elevation, urbanization, historical cropland, road/stream distance and climate (habitat likelihood-ratio chi2=58.5, p=2.7e-9), with the habitat ranking essentially unchanged (Spearman rho=0.98 vs unadjusted); low elevation and higher rainfall additionally raise occurrence whereas urbanization alone is non-significant once habitat is accounted for. Complementing this, gardening is the dominant modern introduction pathway regionally (75% of 1985-2019 introductions, up from 17% pre-1500) and the most species-rich pathway in the province (42 of 81 species), yet gardening-introduced species are individually less widespread than unintentionally introduced ones -- occupying fewer habitat types even after phylogenetic correction (PGLS coefficient -1.10, t=-2.54, p=0.013). This indicates that many gardening taxa are largely confined to a handful of anthropized and moist habitat types (urban, wetland, coastal, riparian) rather than acting as broad habitat generalists.

Caveats: (1) 'prevalence' is operationalized as plot-level occupancy (assumption; the source study's own metric is unknown); (2) minor residual spatial autocorrelation in model residuals (Moran's I ~0.01-0.03, permutation p<0.05); (3) relative-dominance values in forests are based on very few non-native records and are small-sample artifacts, so the occupancy metric is preferred; (4) the adjusted model includes collinear elevation-temperature terms (VIF up to 18.5), mitigated by a drop-temperature stability check that gave identical conclusions; (5) observational cross-sectional design, so associations are not causal.
